    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L16-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Populations of Two Countries",
      "SourceExcerpt": "From the equations, we can tell which colony had a greater initial population (carpenter ants, 8.1 thousand) and which had a greater growth factor (red wood ants, \\(e^{(0.05)}\\)). Will the colony of red wood ants eventually exceed that of…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L16-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Populations of Two Countries",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The intersection of the graphs tells us that about 20 months after the study began, the two colonies have the same population, about 15 thousand. After that point, the population of red wood ants is greater than that of carpenter ants. To…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L16-ACTIVITY-022",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Populations of Two Countries",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Another way to find the point of intersection is using the equations for the functions. At the point of intersection of the graphs, the two functions have the same \\(y\\)-value, so we can write the equation \\(8.1 \\boldcdot e^{(0.03x)}=5.4…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L16-ACTIVITY-023",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Populations of Two Countries",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle \\begin {align} 8.1 \\cdot e^{(0.03x)} &= 5.4 \\cdot e^{(0.05x)}\\\\ \\frac{8.1}{5.4}&=\\frac{e^{(0.05x)}}{e^{(0.03x)}}\\\\ 1.5&=e^{(0.02x)}\\\\ \\ln (1.5)&= 0.02x\\\\ \\frac{0.405}{0.02}& \\approx x\\\\ 20.3& \\approx x\\\\ \\end {align}\\)",
